Compile a unit read from TASTy with the language settings it was compiled with

A unit compiled with capture checking, or with pure functions, enabled by a
command-line option rather than by a language import, carries no trace of it
in its trees, and a run which recompiled it from TASTy went by the options it
was given itself. The phases after capture checking go by the setting
(`UncacheGivenAliases`, the pattern matcher, the inliner), so without the
option repeated the unit was compiled to other code than from source: a given
alias that was cached became a plain method. A run over a classpath mixing
units compiled with and without the option could not be given one set of
options that suited both.

The unit's TASTy attributes record both settings. `ReadTasty` now restores
them on the unit, and marks capture checking as enabled somewhere in the run,
as the option would. `UncacheGivenAliases` keeps a given alias cached when
capture checking is on anywhere in a run from source, since a checked unit can
name the alias of another; a run from TASTy checks no captures, so there it
goes by the unit itself, and each unit of a mixed run gets the code it had.

